2. What the WHEEL links that this tree does not show
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The obligation does not stop at anydoc's own licence. The wheel is a statically linked Rust binary,
so every crate it links ships inside it, and their attribution obligations ship with it. The wheel
carries its own CycloneDX SBOM at

  firecrawl_anydoc-0.2.4.dist-info/sboms/anydoc-python.cyclonedx.json

which names **118 components** under **fifteen distinct SPDX expressions**. That file is the
authority; this section records what it says, because a reader of vendor/anydoc/Cargo.toml sees
eight direct dependencies and would otherwise conclude the obligation is eight rows deep.

  MIT OR Apache-2.0 .......................... 83   the bulk of the Rust ecosystem
  MIT ........................................ 10   anydoc, cfb, lopdf, nom, pdf-inspector, ...
  Unlicense OR MIT ............................ 9   BurntSushi's crates (csv, aho-corasick, jiff)
  Apache-2.0 OR MIT ........................... 5
  (Apache-2.0 OR MIT) AND BSD-3-Clause ........ 1   encoding_rs -- the AND binds BSD-3-Clause
  (MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND Unicode-3.0 ......... 1   unicode-ident -- the AND binds Unicode-3.0
  Apache-2.0 OR BSL-1.0 ....................... 1   ryu
  Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception .............. 1   target-lexicon (build-time)
  Apache-2.0 .................................. 1   zopfli
  Apache-2.0  OR  MIT ......................... 1   fnv (upstream's own double spacing)
  0BSD OR MIT OR Apache-2.0 ................... 1   adler2
  MIT OR Apache-2.0 OR Zlib ................... 1   tinyvec_macros
  Zlib OR Apache-2.0 OR MIT ................... 1   tinyvec
  MIT OR Zlib OR Apache-2.0 ................... 1   miniz_oxide
  Zlib ........................................ 1   zlib-rs -- no disjunct; Zlib binds

Two of those reach omniweave's allowlist (`tools/licences.toml [allow] spdx`, which is
14-security.md section 9.2's list verbatim) and one does not:

  - `Zlib` is on the allowlist. `zlib-rs` offers no alternative and needs none.
  - `BSD-3-Clause` is on the allowlist. `encoding_rs`'s AND is satisfied.
  - **`Unicode-3.0` is NOT on the allowlist**, and `unicode-ident@1.0.24` joins it with AND, so no
    disjunct avoids it. It is a permissive attribution licence over the Unicode character database
    tables and is not a policy problem; it is an allowlist gap, and it is recorded in
    `_plan/_notes/build-defects.md` rather than silently waived here. G3 does not see it: G3 scans
    the PYTHON dependency graph, where this wheel declares one licence -- `MIT` -- and passes.

`Apache-2.0 OR BSL-1.0` (ryu) resolves to Apache-2.0, which is allowlisted; the BSL-1.0 disjunct is
the same id 14-security.md's D9 already records as absent for `torch`, and it binds nothing here.
`Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception` is `target-lexicon`, a build-time crate that does not link into the
shipped binary.

